    "description": "Organization CST Ethiopia Assignment Market Systems Development (MSD) research with recommendations for programme design and strategy Study areas South Omo Zone (South Ethiopia Regional state), Borena zone (Oromia), Tigray region, And Addis Ababa city Contract type/ duration Consultant team or firm (lead consultant + area teams) / maximum 10 weeks from contract signing Reports to Resilient communities programme manager, CST Ethiopia Application deadline Tuesday, November 24,2026, 5:00PM ABOUT CST ETHIOPIA CST Ethiopia represents the overseas development and humanitarian agencies of the Catholic Church from England and Wales (CAFOD), Scotland (SCIAF), and Ireland (Trócaire) in Ethiopia. The three agencies have over 40 years of experience in international development and humanitarian response and are each member of worldwide networks, including Caritas Internationalis. Currently, the three agencies jointly operate a single, integrated country program in Ethiopia through the legal entity CST Ethiopia. BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE Pastoral and agro-pastoral communities in South Omo and Borena, poor urban households in Addis Ababa, and communities recovering from conflict in Tigray face barriers to inclusive livelihoods; weak infrastructure, limited financial services, climate shocks, conflict and displacement. CST is seeking a consultant team to review previous and ongoing MSD interventions by NGOs, government, donors and the private sector in the four areas. And then establish what worked, what did not and why, and turn the lessons into clear, prioritized recommendations for CST programme design and MSD strategy. The study follows the M4P approach (Springfield Center, 2025), Trocaire’s inclusive market systems Approach (2024) and the Green MSD Guidelines (2025). It is women-centered, green and looks at power relations and at how markets depend on rangeland, water and the urban environment. SPECIFIC OBJECTIVES Review MSD interventions in the four areas (the core of the study and the basis of all recommendations). Map how key market systems work: core market, supporting functions, formal and informal rules, and relationships between actors. Trace constraints to the root causes, considering the incentives and capacities of actors who could address them. Assess how culture, geography, gender norms, conflict and climate shocks shape results and benefits; in Addis Ababa informality, access to space and licenses, and safety. In Tigray, assess how MSD and market-based recovery activities were adapted to a post-conflict setting, without creating dependency or distorting markets. Compare the four areas and develop prioritized actionable recommendations for each area (priority market systems, partners, level of facilitation, sequencing, risks, budget implications, indicator of change) and for CST’s MSD strategy. SCOPE OF WORK AND METHODOLOGY A mainly qualitative, participatory, gender and climate-sensitive mixed-methods design using one common framework and core tools in all four areas, adapted to each context. Work includes: desk review of project documents, evaluations, government data, price data and recovery assessments; inception mapping of MSD interventions and agreement with CST on two or three focus market systems; field research (one woreda each in south Omo and Borena, selected sub-cities and markets in Addis Ababa, selected woredas in Tigray) with purposive sampling of traders, brokers, input and service providers, women traders, cooperatives, financial institutions, government offices, customary leaders, implementing staff and participants; tools: key informant interviews, separate FGDs (men, women, youth, poorer HHs), PRA tools, market actor interviews, channel mapping and observation checklist, collected digitally (e.g. kobo Toolbox); analysis with triangulation, a prioritization matrix, and gender, power and “who does/ Who pays” analysis. Where access is restricted (e.g. parts of Tigray), safe alternatives such as local enumerators or phone interviews will be agreed with CST. Ethics: informed consent, confidentiality, CST safeguarding police, conflict-sensitive and do-no-harm practice, and compliance with Ethiopia’s personal data Protection Proclamation No. 1321/2024. DELIVERABLES AND TIMELINE - (Max of 10 weeks with Preliminary Finding Presentation on week 7) Inception report (end of week 1): refined questions, market systems and interventions to review, sampling plan, tools, analysis plan, work plan, safeguarding and risk plan; CST review in week 2, then tool translation and pre-test. Fieldwork (week 3-5) in all four areas using parallel area terms, then a field brief of early findings (end of week 5). Draft report (up to 40 pages excluding annexes), preliminary findings presentation with CST and partners. (Week 7) Final report with Five-page lessons brief and presentation; Programme design and strategy Recommendations Brief (up to 10 pages per area); and data materials (summary of data used, KII/FGD notes, editable market maps). CST will give written comments withing 5 working days of each deliverable.",
